"Spinning Up a Pulsar | VirtualDemos",
"Spinning Up a Pulsar",
"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Astronomy 2e</em>, §{#} (pulsars and the discovery of neutron stars). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/astronomy-2e. The physics behind it: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (conservation of angular momentum) and §{#} (moment of inertia), and Vol. {#}, §{#} (nuclear sizes and densities). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#} and https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Collapse, with A core {#} km in radius turning once a day; {#} km, once every {#} hours; If the Sun collapsed; Heaviest neutron star known. Lighthouse, with Bell’s first pulsar: one pulse a turn; Both beams cross us: two pulses; The beams miss us. Spin down, with The Crab pulsar; The Vela pulsar; Bell’s first pulsar, slowing.",
"The three buttons above the picture switch modes: <strong>Collapse</strong> (a spinning star’s core shrinks to a neutron star), <strong>Lighthouse</strong> (the beams sweep past us, and the graph below shows the pulses a radio telescope records) and <strong>Spin down</strong> (a real pulsar slowing over thousands of years).",
"<strong>Play</strong> runs the stage; the time scale is written at the top of the picture. Drag the picture to turn the view. Click a slider’s readout (the number beside its name) to type an exact value.",
"The readouts list what the demo works out. <strong>Blind</strong> (in the Lab section) hides the answers, so you can work them out first.",
"<strong>Record reading</strong> stores the setup and the readouts of the mode you are in. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> sends the readings to the table for that mode below; <strong>Clear</strong> starts a new series.",
"The model: the core keeps its spin and its magnetic field lines as it shrinks, with its shape unchanged. The pulse timing is exact for beams of the set width around each magnetic pole. The slowing uses the standard model for a spinning magnet in empty space, with the moment of inertia astronomers assume, and real periods and slowing rates from the ATNF Pulsar Catalogue. The star’s look and the beams’ shape are schematic.",
"Follow a dying star’s core as it collapses into a neutron star: find how fast it ends up spinning, how strong its magnetic field and how dense it becomes, then see why we see it as a pulsar, or don’t, and how it slows down.",
"<strong>A city sized nucleus.</strong> When the iron core of a heavy star collapses, its electrons are crushed into its protons, and what is left is a ball of neutrons about {#} km across, holding more than the Sun’s mass: a <strong>neutron star</strong>. Its average density is",
"\\[\\rho = \\frac{M}{\\tfrac43\\pi R^3},\\]",
"about the density of an atomic nucleus, \\(2.7\\times10^{17}\\) kg/m³. Neutrons resist being packed any tighter, the way electrons do in a white dwarf, and that holds the star up.",
"<strong>Spin up.</strong> Every star’s core turns, slowly. As it shrinks it keeps its spin (its angular momentum), like a skater pulling in her arms: it turns faster. If its radius shrinks by a factor \\(f = R_0/R\\), it turns \\(f^2\\) times faster, so the time for one turn, the <strong>spin period</strong>, goes down by \\(f^2\\):",
"\\[P = \\frac{P_0}{f^2}.\\]",
"The same squeeze packs the star’s magnetic field lines together, so the field grows by the same factor: \\(B = B_0 f^2\\). A point on the equator moves at \\(v = 2\\pi R/P\\).",
"<strong>The lighthouse.</strong> A neutron star’s magnetic poles are tilted from its spin axis, as the Earth’s are. Charged particles stream out along the field and send radio beams from both magnetic poles. As the star spins, the beams sweep around the sky like a lighthouse’s. Each time a beam sweeps across the Earth, a radio telescope sees a pulse. Such a star is a <strong>pulsar</strong>, and the time between its pulses is set by its spin.",
"<strong>Slowing down.</strong> A pulsar’s spinning magnet loses energy to the radiation and particles it sends out, so it slows. The rate at which its period grows, \\(\\dot P\\) (seconds per second), is tiny but measurable. The energy it loses each second is what lights up the gas around young pulsars, such as the Crab Nebula. The Sun gives off \\(L_\\odot = 3.83\\times10^{26}\\) W.",
"\\[M_\\odot = 1.99\\times10^{30}\\ \\text{kg},\\qquad 1\\ \\text{day} = 86{,}400\\ \\text{s},\\qquad 1\\ \\text{yr} = 3.156\\times10^{7}\\ \\text{s}.\\]",
"A neutron star has \\(2.0\\ M_\\odot\\) and a radius of {#} km. Find its density. How does it compare with an atomic nucleus, and with a white dwarf such as Sirius B (\\(2.4\\times10^{9}\\) kg/m³)?",
"A star’s core of radius {#} km turns once a day. It collapses to a neutron star of radius {#} km. By what factor does it shrink? What is its new spin period, in seconds?",
"The Crab pulsar spins about {#} times a second and has a radius of about {#} km. What is its spin period, and how fast does its equator move?",
"A neutron star spins {#} times a second and sends out two strong radio beams, one from each magnetic pole. Will a radio telescope on Earth always see pulses from it? If it does, how many pulses will it see each turn? Write your answer and one sentence of reasoning before you open the demo.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Collapse.</strong> The demo opens in <strong>Collapse</strong> with the setup <strong>A core {#} km in radius turning once a day</strong>: a core of {#} \\(M_\\odot\\) with a field of {#} T, shrinking to {#} km. The slider <strong>Collapse so far</strong> says how far it has gone. Click its readout and type {#}, then press <strong>Record reading</strong>. Do the same at {#}, {#}, {#}, {#} and {#}%. Then press the setup <strong>{#} km, once every {#} hours</strong>, set <strong>Collapse so far</strong> to {#} the same way and press <strong>Record reading</strong>. That makes seven readings. Press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong>: they go into Table {#}. Press <strong>Play</strong> on either setup to watch a collapse.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Lighthouse.</strong> Press <strong>Clear</strong>. Switch to <strong>Lighthouse</strong>. Press the setup <strong>Bell’s first pulsar: one pulse a turn</strong>, press <strong>Play</strong>, and watch the beams sweep past the Earth and the pulses on the graph below. Press <strong>Record reading</strong>. Do the same for <strong>Both beams cross us: two pulses</strong> and <strong>The beams miss us</strong>. Then, with <strong>The beams miss us</strong> still chosen, click the readout beside <strong>Our line of sight ζ (from the spin axis)</strong>, type {#} and press <strong>Record reading</strong>. That makes four readings. Press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong>: they go into Table {#}. Count the pulses you saw in each turn of each setup and write the counts down.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Spin down.</strong> Press <strong>Clear</strong>. Switch to <strong>Spin down</strong>. Press <strong>The Crab pulsar</strong>, <strong>The Vela pulsar</strong> and <strong>Bell’s first pulsar, slowing</strong> in turn, and press <strong>Record reading</strong> after each: each opens at the pulsar’s age today. That makes three readings. Press <strong>Export to worksheet</strong>: they go into Table {#}. Then press <strong>The Crab pulsar</strong> again and <strong>Play</strong> to watch it slow over the next tens of thousands of years.",
"<strong>Table {#}. Collapse.</strong> Six stages of the {#} km core, then the {#} km core at the end. Type \\(f^2\\) yourself.",
